Let's denote the $6$ vertices of the hexagon by A B C D E F in clockwise order. First, arbitrarily place the red bead in A. There are $6$ cases, in terms of the placements of the yellow bead, as shown below (please notice that all black dots are equivalent because of the symmetry): 
[asy]
size(200);
defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));
pair A=(30,80),B=A+(-10,30),C=B+(-10,-30),D=C+(10,0),E=D+(30,0),F=E+(-10,30);
pair A1=(100,80),B1=A1+(-40,30),C1=B1+(-40,-30),D1=C1+(40,0),E1=D1+(50,0),F1=E1+(-40,30);
pair[] dotted={A,B,C,D,E,F};
pair[] dotted1={A1,B1,C1,D1,E1,F1};
for(int i=1;i<=5;++i)
draw(A..(i-1)*dir(60)..B--cycle,dotted+linewidth(.4));
draw(A1..(i-1)*dir(60)..B1--cycle,dotted1+linewidth(.4));

drawOrig:=annulus((0,0),10,300,600);
for(int y=-2;++y && draw(3.5+300*y<--330+70*dir(240-60*y));)
}
label(" $\rm case 1$ ",(100,72));
draw(A1..(5)*dir(60)..B1--cycle);
label(" $\rm case 2$ ",(100,69));
draw((A+(-10,30)).arc((30,80),10,0--70));
label(" $\rm case 3$ ",(73,89));
draw((B+(-10,-30)).arc((30,80),10,100--170));
label(" $\rm case 4$ ",(100,65));
draw(A..(4)*dir(60)..B);
label(" $\rm case 5$ ",(100,62));
draw(A1..(4)*dir(60)..B1);
[/asy]
[asy]
size(200);
defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));
pair A=(30,80),B=A+(-10,30),C=B+(-10,-30),D=C+(10,0),E=D+(30,0),F=E+(-10,30);
pair A1=(100,80),B1=A1+(-40,30),C1=B1+(-40,-30),D1=C1+(40,0),E1=D1+(50,0),F1=E1+(-40,30);
draw(A..(0)*dir(60)..B--cycle,dotted);
draw(A1..(0)*dir(60)..B1--cycle,dotted1);
draw((A+(-10,30)).arc((30,80),10,0--70));
[/asy]
Notice that if we reflect this arrangement across BD, it is equivalent to case 4, and if we reflect it across BE, it is equivalent to case 5, as indicated by green and black lines on the figure.
Comment: What is the most essential part? Reflection is more significant than rotation. The partial result is that, since there are $3 \times 2!$ ways to place the yellow bead and the red bead in vertex A and B, respectively, the answer should be $\frac{6 \times 3 \times 2!}{6}=6$ and hence one may conjecture that for a general hexagon grid, the answer is $n!$. However, since there is a more essential symmetry since there is an inherent symmetry of the hexagon (in addition to the symmetry self-pair is reflected), such symmetry breaks the symmetry of rotations as that distinct rotations will pair every one of them to other distinct rotations, except the rotation of $120^\circ$, $240^\circ$, and the identity. Since the rotations of $0^\circ$ and $360^\circ$ are the same, the answer to the general case is $\frac{6!}{4}=360$.
